# Every way an arbiter can be booked a judicial mistake, and the log each one
# leaves for a reader to find it by.
#
# Checked by: ./script/check-subgraph-arbiter-events.sh
# Rule:       every caller of ArbiterRegistryFacet._recordArbiterMistake has a
#             record here, and every record names at least one event that the
#             subgraph actually indexes.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Owner decision 15a: an arbiter accused by the chain must
# see EVERY dispute the accusation stands on. The accusation names one and
# stands on three, so the rest are recovered from the logs — which only works
# while every path that books a mistake leaves a log naming the arbiter. On
# 21 August 2026 one of the three did not, and nothing noticed: the timeout
# booked its mistake in silence for two days.
#
# THE SCENE IS NOT ENOUGH, and that is the whole reason for a second mechanism.
# test_TheWholeRunIsRecoverableFromLogsWithATimeoutInIt plays a run of three and
# demands all three be readable — but it plays the three paths it KNOWS. A
# fourth caller added tomorrow is never acted, so the scene stays green; and if
# it is added as a branch inside a function that already exists, no selector
# moves either, so the locks over the cut's selector set stay green with it.
# The scene proves the paths it plays. This file is what makes the LIST of paths
# a thing somebody has to touch.
#
# ⚠️ NOT A SCANNER OVER THE SOURCE, and the distinction is the one this
# repository has been burned by twice ("the sixth way": a lock that recognises
# spelling and goes quiet when the same thing is written another way). The
# callers are taken from solc's AST, by the id the call resolves to — so
# `_recordArbiterMistake` reached through a wrapper shows up as the WRAPPER
# being a new caller, which is exactly right, and a mention in a comment shows
# up as nothing at all.
#
# FORMAT. A record is
#
#     === <Contract>.<function> ===
#
# followed by a line beginning literally with
#
#     log:
#
# naming one or more indexed events, comma separated, and a line beginning
# literally with
#
#     recoverable, because:
#
# whose text continues until the next header. A record missing either line makes
# the gate treat the file as damaged and refuse — the same deliberate
# inconvenience as script/gasless-sender.allow and
# script/subgraph-arbiter-events.allow.
#
# ⚠️ THE `log:` NAMES ARE CHECKED AGAINST THE HANDLER MAP, not merely against
# the contracts. Naming an event nobody indexes would satisfy the letter of this
# file and leave the reader exactly where he was.


=== ArbiterRegistryFacet.overturnVerdict ===
log: VerdictOverturned
recoverable, because: the log names the arbiter in its own right —
  VerdictOverturned(address indexed agreement, address indexed arbiter, bool) —
  so the dispute and the man are in one place and a reader filters by either.
  This is the only path of the three that never needed anything added.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet.notifyArbiterTimeout ===
log: ArbiterTimeoutRecorded
recoverable, because: it names the arbiter in its own right, and it exists only
  because this list was written down and found short.
  ⚠️ THE PATH THAT WAS SILENT. Until 21 August 2026 this function emitted
  nothing at all, and the neighbouring Agreement.ArbiterTimedOut names the
  CLIENT — a name that reads like the arbiter's and is not. A mistake run
  containing a timeout came out a dispute short, and the accused was shown two
  of the three things he was about to be removed over.
  The event and the booking sit in one call frame, which Agreement reaches
  inside an empty try/catch: they revert together or not at all, so the counter
  cannot count a mistake this log cannot name.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet.resolveAppeal ===
log: AppealResolved, VerdictSubmitted
recoverable, because: NO log names the judged arbiter on this path — the panel's
  overturn emits no VerdictOverturned at all, AppealResolved carries the
  appellant, and AppealVoteCast carries the VOTER in a parameter that is
  actually named `arbiter`. Two logs are therefore needed and both are indexed:
  AppealResolved gives the dispute, and the verdict of that dispute gives whose
  it was. That is why VerdictSubmitted is indexed even though nothing on a card
  asks for it.
